ping and tax gathering, and military organization and methods of repressing dissent were crucial to the story. But Strayer also argued that “real loyalty is based neither on fear nor on self-interest. There must be genuine respect, admiration, and, if possible, love for the object of loyalty.” In other words, the governmental machinery in place by the time of Philip IV could not have done its work without the development, dissemination, and popular embrace of a royal ideology that linked Capetian kings and their subjects in the belief that France was a new “Holy Land,” inhabited by a “Chosen People,” and ruled over by God’s “Most Christian” king.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 942, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 841})
